[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Composition over Inheritance and Interface Segregation"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-oriented design relies heavily on how classes and objects relate to one another. Two fundamental principles that guide robust software architecture are Composition over Inheritance and the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP). Mastering these concepts prevents tight coupling, rigid class hierarchies, and bloated interfaces."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem with Deep Inheritance Hierarchies"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance (the "is-a" relationship) can be extremely useful, but it is frequently abused. When developers build deep inheritance trees, changes at the base class level can inadvertently break derived classes, leading to the fragile base class problem. Furthermore, it forces rigid taxonomies that struggle to adapt to new business requirements."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Composition: The "Has-a" Relationship"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Composition advocates for building complex types by combining smaller, focused objects rather than inheriting behaviors. By favoring composition (the "has-a" relationship), systems become more dynamic, loosely coupled, and easier to test in isolation."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class Engine {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Engine started.");
    }
}

class Car {
    private Engine engine;

    public Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }

    public void drive() {
        engine.start();
        System.out.println("Car is moving.");
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Interface Segregation"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Interface Segregation Principle (the "I" in SOLID) states that no client should be forced to depend on methods it does not use. Instead of one massive interface containing many methods, it is better to create several client-specific interfaces."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fat interfaces force implementing classes to write empty or dummy methods for functionality they do not support."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Segregated interfaces ensure high cohesion and a clear contract tailored to specific consumer needs."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring Example: Segregating Interfaces"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where a single Worker interface forces simple workers to implement management tasks. We can segregate this into distinct interfaces."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Feedable {
    void eat();
}

class HumanWorker implements Workable, Feedable {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Human working.");
    }
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Human eating lunch.");
    }
}

class RobotWorker implements Workable {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Robot working continuously.");
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Composition over Inheritance with the Interface Segregation Principle, software engineers build flexible, maintainable, and highly scalable systems. Codebases shift from rigid, monolithic structures to modular networks of interchangeable components."}]
